WEBVTT

00:00:01.679 --> 00:00:08.679
Hi guys, my name is Andrea, I'm from Mexico, and I really do love the episode about the limo.
大家好，我叫安德烈娅，来自墨西哥，我真的很喜欢关于加长林肯那一集。

00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:19.679
I've actually watched it every single New Year's Eve, right after 12, and honestly the funny thing is New Year's Eve is like my least favorite holiday of all.
实际上我每个除夕夜都会看，就在12点刚过之后，说实话，有趣的是，除夕夜其实是我最不喜欢的节日。

00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:53.219
But I don't know, this episode just says to me what's important about a holiday, you know, like it's not about what party you go to or about gifts or about, you know, you know, make it extraordinary, but about the people you actually spend it with, about reflecting, about being thankful for one another, and I don't know, I guess that's just one of the things that I love the most about the show, that it makes you come back to the people you love and making sure you spend the most time as possible, you can with them and cherish each other.
但怎么说呢，这一集对我而言讲述了节日真正重要的意义，你知道的，它无关乎你去了哪个派对，无关乎礼物，也无关乎把它弄得多么盛大，而是关于你真正和谁一起度过，关于反思，关于彼此感恩，怎么说呢，我想这也是我最喜欢这部剧的原因之一，它让你回到你爱的人身边，确保你尽可能多地花时间陪伴他们，并彼此珍惜。

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:59.060
Thank you so much for doing this podcast, this is amazing, so thank you and thank you for changing my life for the better.
非常感谢你们做这个播客，这太棒了，谢谢你们，谢谢你们让我的生活变得更好。

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:11.640
I'm alone, won't be soon, in New York City, when I see you.
我孤身一人，但很快就不会了，在纽约市，当我见到你时。

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:19.480
Please permit me to tell you everything, York City.
请允许我对你诉说一切，纽约市。

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.120
Well, hello, welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Mother.
好了，大家好，欢迎收看《老爸老妈的浪漫史》制作内幕的又一期节目。

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.319
I'm Josh Radner, and I am joined by my friend Craig Thomas.
我是乔什·拉德纳，和我一起的是我的朋友克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:28.400
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.400
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.599
Hi.
嗨。

00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:34.667
I almost cry every time at the openings now that I'm producer, Alec puts together.
现在由我们的制片人亚历克剪辑的片头，每次看到我都差点哭出来。

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.799
I literally almost start to cry every time.
我真的每次都差点哭出来。

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:39.898
Andrea, I kind of thought we could just play that letter.
安德烈娅，我刚刚在想，我们可以直接播放那封信。

00:01:39.898 --> 00:01:43.215
It's like, that's pretty much that's the episode in some way.
这感觉就像是，这在某种程度上就是那一集的缩影。

00:01:43.215 --> 00:01:44.084
She sends it up.
她总结得很到位。

00:01:44.084 --> 00:01:46.639
So thank you for joining us on today's episode.
所以谢谢大家收听今天的节目。

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.255
It was just to be the shortest one.
本来这应该会是最短的一集。

00:01:48.255 --> 00:01:51.901
So if you're just tuning in, Andrea from Mexico, which I just love that letter.
所以如果你刚开始收听，来自墨西哥的安德烈娅——我真的很喜欢那封信。

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:53.516
Thank you so much for that, Andrea.
安德烈娅，非常感谢你。

00:01:53.516 --> 00:01:54.016
Me too.
我也是。

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.226
She was talking about episode 11 of how I met your mother.
她谈论的是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》第11集。

00:01:57.226 --> 00:02:04.701
I'm getting confused about titles, Like I say, how I made your mother, how we made your mother very tricky, but on this podcast.
我总是把标题搞混，就像我说的，How I made your mother（我如何制作你母亲），How we made your mother（我们如何制作你母亲），这在这个播客里很容易口误。

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:09.606
We are doing a rewatch I played Ted Mosby on the show from what was it 2005 to 2014?
我们正在重温这部剧，我在剧中饰演泰德·莫斯底，从什么时候开始的？2005年到2014年？

00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:19.766
I was in every single episode Craig Thomas Probably had a line or two or the whole every line in many of the episodes he co-created the show I got in two jokes in the series.
我出演了每一集，克雷格·托马斯可能在很多集里有一两句台词，或者包办了所有的台词，他和卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧，我在剧中贡献了两个笑梗，我贡献了两个笑梗。

00:02:19.766 --> 00:02:37.343
I got two jokes He co-created the show with Carter Bayes And we're working our way through it and we're unpacking this mystery of why the show has endured why people love it so much, why people watch the limo episode every New Year's Eve, and what the show just has done to people and continues to do.
他和卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧，我们正在重温并解开这个谜团：为什么这部剧能经久不衰，为什么人们这么爱它，为什么人们每个除夕夜都要看林肯车那一集，以及这部剧对人们产生了怎样的影响并仍在产生影响。

00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:44.000
But before we start today, Craig, talking about the limo episode 11, I did want to do a little State of the Union.
但在我们今天开始讨论第11集林肯车之前，克雷格，我想做一个小小的国情咨文。

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.120
And I just wanted to ask you, how's it going doing this podcast for you?
我只想问你，对你来说，做这个播客感觉怎么样？

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:47.997
Oh, I love it.
哦，我太喜欢了。

00:02:47.997 --> 00:03:00.599
And you've chosen the perfect moment to do State of the Union, because this is episode 11 out of 22, almost all of the seasons were either, all the seasons were either 22 episodes or 24, 24 in success.
而且你选择做国情咨文的时机简直太完美了，因为这是22集里的第11集，几乎所有的季度要么是22集，要么是24集，大热之后是24集。

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:04.080
Once we became sort of more of a hit, they're like, give us a couple extra a year.
一旦我们成了热门剧集，他们就会说，一年多给我们拍几集吧。

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.800
And that was a very good sign when I went out to 24.
当我拍到24集的时候，这是一个非常好的迹象。

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.240
And one year because of the writer's strike in 2007, it was only 20.
而且有那么一年，因为2007年的编剧罢工，只有20集。

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.560
But so right now we're at the exact midpoint and I am loving it.
但所以现在我们正处于正中间的位置，我非常喜欢它。

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:26.319
I have not watched a lot of these episodes in years, getting to watch it and sort of share it with fans and here like we just did that amazing opening of this episode, you hear what it means to people.
其中很多集我多年都没看过了，现在能重新看并与粉丝分享，就像我们刚刚播放的那段精彩片头一样，听到它对人们意味着什么。

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:27.879
I just find it's so moving.
我发现这太感人了。

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:29.439
It's just, I feel like I'm watching it.
我感觉就像是在观看它。

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:31.159
I feel like a different person watching it.
我觉得自己像个不同的人在观看它。

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:33.400
I was so close to it when we were making it.
当年制作的时候我离它太近了。

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.280
I saw it through such different eyes.
我是通过完全不同的视角来看待它的。

00:03:36.479 --> 00:03:37.479
And I'm loving it.
而我爱极了这种感觉。

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:38.319
I'm loving it.
我太喜欢了。

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:44.800
It's wonderful and sort of, I'm marveling at what we did 20 years ago, right?
这真是奇妙，某种程度上，我在惊叹我们20年前做过的事，对吧？

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.159
I can't sort of can't believe a lot of it.
我简直有点不敢相信其中的很多内容。

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.159
I'm like, how do we do that?
我在想，我们当时是怎么做到的？

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:48.039
We didn't know what we're doing.
我们当时根本不知道自己在做什么。

00:03:48.039 --> 00:03:53.960
I think when you're in it and you're close to it and an episode's airing six weeks after you just made it.
我想，当你身处其中并且离得很近，一集节目在你刚制作完六周后就会播出。

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:59.280
Yeah, for me, my ego was just tied up in it in a different way than it is now.
是的，对当时的我和现在的我来说，我的自我（ego）与它的绑定方式是完全不同的。

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:09.000
I watch it with so much more forgiveness and I'm marveling at the fact that I was involved in this charming show that continues to delight people.
我现在以多得多的包容心态来看待它，并为自己曾参与这部能继续带给人们快乐的迷人剧集而感到惊叹。

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:26.079
And I just told you, I've just gotten back from the one leg of my tour and I'm meeting all of these people who just, the waitress at the restaurant restaurant I was at last night said, you I got my whole sense of humor from you.
我刚跟你说过，我刚结束巡演的一段行程，我遇到了形形色色的人，比如我昨晚去的那家餐厅的服务员说，你懂的，我的幽默感全是从你身上学来的。

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:30.399
It's what she told me, which that's so cool.
这是她告诉我的，太酷了。

00:04:30.399 --> 00:04:31.264
I said, well, let's see.
我说，好吧，我们拭目以待。

00:04:31.264 --> 00:04:32.199
So let's see what you got.
那让我们看看你有什么本事。

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.020
I'll be the judge of that.
我来评判。

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:34.920
Yeah, let's do it.
行，我们干吧。

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.680
But, you know, I was thinking sometimes I think that there's a tendency to dismiss TV.
但是，你知道，我在想，有时候人们往往会看轻电视剧。

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:59.812
And I've been guilty of this too, as a kind of a popular medium that maybe, well, I think, you know, there's been so much quality television in almost novelistic ways, our show, the wire, the, you know, the sopranos, like, TV has really been elevated to high art, but it's also a populist medium, right?
我也曾犯过这样的错误，把它当作一种通俗媒介，也许，嗯，我觉得，你看，现在有那么多高质量的电视剧，几乎像小说一样，我们的剧、《火线》、《黑道家族》之类的，电视确实已经被提升为高级艺术，但它也是一种大众媒介，对吧？

00:04:59.812 --> 00:05:03.221
It's in everyone's home and now pockets, living rooms.
它在每个人的家里，现在还在口袋里、客厅里。

00:05:03.221 --> 00:05:18.375
But, you know, if you think about our need, our human need for story, I'm sure people were, you know, stopping Sophocles on the street in the agora or whatever, and being like, yeah, I don't remember what he wrote, but you know what I mean?
但是，你知道，如果你想想我们的需求，我们对故事的人类需求，我敢肯定人们过去常常在阿哥拉广场或其他地方拦住索福克勒斯，然后说，是的，我不记得他写了什么，但你知道我的意思吗？

00:05:18.375 --> 00:05:22.480
Like saying Like smoking in the banded too, that's what he wrote.
就像说，就像在《班台》里抽烟一样，那是他写的。

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.959
But like saying, you know, stopping a playwright, greasing the Orestire Trilogy really floored me.
但就像说，你知道，拦住一个剧作家，说《俄瑞斯忒斯三部曲》真的震撼到我了。

00:05:27.959 --> 00:05:29.519
But I've got a few notes.
不过我有一些意见要提。

00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:30.959
But I've got a few notes, yeah.
不过我有一些意见要提，对。

00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:35.600
Can you sign just, yeah, Elektra on my arm?
你能在我的胳膊上签个名吗，就签，对，埃勒克特拉？

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.759
Cause I'm gonna get a hat to you.
因为我要去纹个身。

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:40.360
That's what people want me to write people while they answer other arms they get a tattoo.
人们就是想让我写在他们身上，当他们撸起袖子时就会去纹身。

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:46.240
But I think there is a human, I really maintain, there's these human ache for stories.
但我认为人类有一种天性，我一直坚持认为，人类对故事有着强烈的渴望。

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.639
We need them.
我们需要它们。

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:55.160
And when people say to me, like, this show changed my life, it taught me how to talk with my dad.
当人们对我说，这部剧改变了我的生活，它教我如何与我父亲沟通。

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.959
It got me through my divorce.
它陪我度过了离婚的难关。

00:05:56.959 --> 00:05:59.920
It, you know, gave me my sense of humor.
它，你知道，给了我幽默感。

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.199
I take that really seriously now.
我现在非常严肃地看待这一点。

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:06.240
And I really, I continue to be moved to have been a part of something that affected people this way.
而且我真的，我依然为自己能参与到这样一部触动人们的作品中而感动。

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:15.839
But I also, you know, it's so strange to be watching this with my wife and watching almost every episode with her, except this episode we were separate because I was on tour.
但我也，你知道，和我妻子一起看这个，几乎每一集都和她一起看，这种感觉太奇怪了，除了这一集我们分开了，因为我在巡演。

00:06:15.839 --> 00:06:24.959
but just to look back, it's weird when you do something on camera for this long.
但只要回想一下，当你把生活中的某些事在镜头前做这么久，感觉真的很奇妙。

00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:30.759
It's almost like you have this weird moving in both senses of the word, like photo album of your life.
这几乎就像你有一本在某种意义上触动人心的生活相册。

00:06:30.759 --> 00:06:33.600
It's this weird documentary snapshot.
这是一个奇特的纪录片快照。

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.199
And even though that wasn't what was going on in my life, it was partly what was going on in my life.
尽管那并不是我生活的全部，但它确实是我生活的一部分。

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:38.759
At least that was my job.
至少那是我的工作。

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:43.279
But most people don't have their jobs filmed and then be in doubt to the rest of the world.
但大多数人的工作不会被拍摄下来，然后向全世界展示。

00:06:43.279 --> 00:06:44.660
It just feels so weird.
这种感觉太奇怪了。

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.540
I think about this from your perspective in this rewatch so often.
在这部剧的重温过程中，我经常从你的角度来思考这个问题。

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.259
I'm like, well, Josh knows where he was that day.
我想，哦，乔恩知道他那一天在哪里。

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:52.259
That's him popping out of the top of a limo.
那是他从豪华轿车顶部探出头来。

00:06:52.259 --> 00:06:56.459
He's like, that's what I was doing that day of work of my life 20 years ago.
他会想，那是我20岁时生命中工作的那一天所做的事情。

00:06:56.459 --> 00:06:58.519
No one gets that and that's you.
没有人能有这种体验，而那就是你。

00:06:58.519 --> 00:07:06.060
That's you at that age, at that minute, existing on planet Earth in that moment and watching it again and kind of going back into that.
那是你在那个年龄、那一分钟、在那个时刻存在于地球上的你，再次观看它，并某种程度上回到那个过去。

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:14.060
Like I'm having a little bit of that kind of vertigo of like, I remember I was standing off to the side at Video Village watching the four cameras, you know, the four monitors showing the four camera shots.
就像我有点那种眩晕感，就像，我记得我站在视频村旁边，看着四个摄像机，你知道，四个监视器显示四个摄像机画面。

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:14.899
But what is it?
但那是什么？

00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:22.620
Is there kind of like a weird emotional vertigo of trying to like remember your way back into like, what it looks like, say within that limo?
这是否有一种奇怪的情感眩晕感，试图回忆并找回当时在豪华轿车里的那种感觉？

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.779
That just occurred to me.
我刚刚突然想到了这一点。

00:07:24.779 --> 00:07:36.819
I remember, I think sometimes when you're in the apartment or you're in the bar, we did so much in there that it's hard to isolate, oh, I remember that day, unless something really particularly strange happened.
我记得，有时候当你在公寓里或者在酒吧里时，我们在那里拍了太多戏，以至于很难分清，哦，我记得那天发生的事，除非发生了什么特别奇怪的事。

00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:42.800
but the limo became shorthand for this prison that we all shared together.
但那辆豪华轿车成了我们共同经历的这座监狱的代名词。

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:51.240
Like I remember us saying limo like, whenever it was going on, we'd be like, well, at least we're not in the limo because it was so intense.
我记得我们提到豪华轿车时，每当有戏要拍，我们就会说，好吧，至少我们不用待在豪华轿车里，因为那太压抑了。

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:57.000
And I think did it remind me, was it all shot in an actual limo or was some of it tricked out like to look like a limo?
我想，它让我想起，它是全在真正的豪华轿车里拍的，还是有些是伪装成豪华轿车搭的景？

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.199
Did we shoot some of it on the stage?
我们有些镜头是在摄影棚里拍的吗？

00:07:58.199 --> 00:07:59.040
I can't remember.
我记不清了。

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.600
Well, it was an episode that Carter was on the set for and he would probably have more memories than me.
嗯，那是卡特在片场的一集，他可能比我有更多的记忆。

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.959
So I wish we had him on this one.
所以我希望我们能请他来聊聊这一集。

00:08:04.959 --> 00:08:09.300
But I think, no, I think we were shooting the inside of the limo.
但我想，不，我想我们当时是在豪华轿车的内部拍摄的。

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.879
I think a lot of it was really in that limo, I think, and it was really hard to shoot.
我觉得很大程度上，真的就是在那个豪华轿车里，我觉得，而且真的很难拍。

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:13.720
It was cramped.
里面空间很逼仄。

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:18.379
I mean, a lot of times there were like four people in like a three-person back seat.
我的意思是，很多时候后座明明只能坐三个人，却挤了四个人。

00:08:18.379 --> 00:08:27.000
I do have very strong memories of, one thing I really loved was how the sunroof kind of area becomes this other playing space.
我确实有很深刻的记忆，我非常喜欢的一点是，天窗附近的那块区域如何变成了一个别样的表演空间。

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.079
It's like another room.
它就像是另一个房间。

00:08:28.079 --> 00:08:30.879
Well, it's like stepping out onto the balcony, right?
嗯，就像是走到阳台上一样，对吧？

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:40.440
Like it has that feel, and I just love, I remember very strongly like what it was joyful to get out of the limo, kind of get some air and pop up.
就那种感觉，我真的很喜欢，我记忆犹新的是，从轿车里钻出来透透气、把头探出去是一件多么让人开心的事情。

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.879
But I really liked all the scenes that were played up there.
但我真的很喜欢所有在上面演的戏。

00:08:42.879 --> 00:08:58.960
