我也喜欢，我们之前讨论过这个，但我太喜欢天窗作为另一个表演空间了。

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:34.059
And I just enjoy, there was something so pleasurable, maybe it was just, I got to leave the limo, but there was something so nice about popping up and having a scene up there.
而且我很享受，那种感觉太美妙了，也许只是因为我终于能离开林肯轿车了，但探出头去并在上面演一场戏，这种感觉真的很棒。

00:39:34.059 --> 00:39:39.940
And also we had talked about this when Mary Beth says she can tell Robbins into him and he says, wait, really?
还有，我们之前讨论过这个，当玛丽·贝丝说她看得出罗宾对泰德有意时，泰德说，等等，真的吗？

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:43.760
And it occurred to me, like, Ted is a bad actor.
我突然意识到，泰德是个烂演员。

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:47.679
Like, I hope I'm a good actor, but Ted is a bad actor.
就像，我希望我是个好演员，但泰德是个烂演员。

00:39:47.679 --> 00:39:50.199
He's not a master of disguise.
他可不是什么易容大师。

00:39:50.199 --> 00:39:56.480
Like Barney's a pretty good actor and most of his life is somewhat of an act or a lot of it.
就像巴尼是个相当不错的演员，他的大部分生活都带着点演戏的成分，或者很大程度上都是。

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:57.480
Yes.
是的。

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:00.840
But Ted is just, he's, he's a guile-less, is that the word?
但泰德就是，他是个毫无心机的人，是这个词吗？

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:01.840
Like he's...
就像他……

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:02.840
He's guile-less.
他毫无心机。

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:03.840
Yeah.
对。

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:04.840
Yeah.
没错。

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:06.639
There's no, there's no, um, there's no smokescreen.
没有，没有任何，嗯，没有任何烟幕弹。

00:40:06.639 --> 00:40:07.639
There's nothing.
什么都没有。

00:40:07.639 --> 00:40:08.639
He's not masking.
他没有伪装。

00:40:08.639 --> 00:40:13.199
He can't help himself but ask his date if she really thinks Robin likes him.
他情不自禁地问约会对象，她是不是真的觉得罗宾喜欢他。

00:40:13.199 --> 00:40:19.079
It's that there's something so, it's sort of shitty and great at the same time because His dad just can't lie.
那种感觉太……可以说是既有点烂透了又棒极了，因为他爸爸就是不会撒谎。

00:40:19.079 --> 00:40:20.440
He can't cover that up.
他掩饰不了。

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:21.480
Yeah, I love that.
对，我太喜欢那个了。

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:24.000
I love that you play that moment, great.
我很高兴你谈到了那个时刻，太棒了。

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:28.440
Speaking of smokescreens, I love Marshall emerging from that steam.
说到烟幕，我好喜欢马修从那团蒸汽中现身。

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:29.599
Oh my God.
噢天呐。

00:40:29.599 --> 00:40:32.199
Lily, I'm going to go call Marshall and she just means calling his name out.
“莉莉，我要去叫马修”，而她其实只是在大声喊他的名字。

00:40:32.199 --> 00:40:45.480
That was, I love when we have those little magic New York moments and that entire city, he can, she can call his name echoing through the buildings through the canyons of New York and he will come running out of the smoke that I love the metaphysical weirdness of that.
那真是，我好喜欢我们有那种富有纽约魔力的神奇时刻，在整座城市里，他能，她能呼喊他的名字，声音在纽约峡谷般的楼宇间回荡，而他会从烟雾中跑出来，我太喜欢那种超现实的奇妙感了。

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:48.039
York is a very strange place.
约克（纽约）是个非常奇特的地方。

00:40:48.039 --> 00:40:53.199
It's this intensely small village and it's one of the biggest cities in the world all at the same time.
它既是一个极度狭小的村庄，同时又是世界上最大城市之一。

00:40:53.199 --> 00:40:54.039
All at the same time.
两者同时存在。

00:40:54.039 --> 00:41:00.360
And there's something like, if you really understand New York and the geography of at least Manhattan, that's impossible.
而且有种说法是，如果你真的了解纽约和地理，至少是曼哈顿的地理，那是不可能的。

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:01.360
It's impossible.
这不可能。

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:03.360
It's too loud, it's too crazy.
太吵了，太疯狂了。

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:08.639
Like, even if he was half a block away, you can't hear someone screaming a block away.
比如说，就算他只在半个街区外，你也不可能听到有人在一个街区外尖叫。

00:41:08.639 --> 00:41:09.480
A new city.
崭新的城市。

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:10.320
A new city.
崭新的城市。

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:11.320
Yeah.
对。

00:41:11.320 --> 00:41:12.559
But it's magic.
但这就是魔力。

00:41:12.559 --> 00:41:17.659
Like, Alec and I talk about this, like just adding magic into scripts.
就像阿历克斯和我讨论过的那样，就是在剧本里加入魔力。

00:41:17.659 --> 00:41:23.900
Like Alec, I've been to tell something you told me but about Fargo speaking of the Coen brothers.
就像阿历克斯，我要说点你告诉过我的事，关于《冰血暴》，说到科恩兄弟。

00:41:23.900 --> 00:41:31.300
You said a female police chief investigating a murder and in, where was it, Minnesota is great.
你说过一个怀孕的女警察局长去调查谋杀案，在，是在哪来着，明尼苏达，这很棒。

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:34.460
But that she's pregnant is magic, right?
但她是个孕妇，这就是魔力，对吧？

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:35.619
Yes, yes.
对，对。

00:41:35.619 --> 00:41:36.539
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:41:36.539 --> 00:41:42.340
So there's just these little details that enchant a script a little bit more and that's one of them.
所以就是有这些小细节，能让剧本多出一层魔幻色彩，这就是其中之一。

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:58.340
Um, uh, I also, uh, oh, I remember Jason loving emerging from, I mean, it's almost like a professional wrestler coming out of the dressing room, like in the fog, like he, there was something he, you could tell he was in the same way, like, okay, awesome, he loved the dance, like, he loved coming out of that.
呃，我，我还，噢，我记得杰森很喜欢那种从……我的意思是，这几乎就像是一个职业摔角手从化妆间走出来，带著烟雾，就像他，有一种他，你能看出他也是这样，好吧，太棒了，他喜欢那个节奏，你知道的，他喜欢那样出场。

00:41:58.340 --> 00:41:59.340
Yeah.
对。

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:00.340
That's now he runs.
那就是他奔跑的方式。

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:01.340
He appears out of the smoke.
他从烟雾中现身。

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:04.340
The Bon Jovi song for the first time becomes full score.
邦·乔维（Bon Jovi）的歌第一次变成了完整的配乐。

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:05.340
It's not being played in the limo.
它不再是在加长轿车里播放。

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:09.340
It's actually the score and it plays him in and it is a very get psyched moment.
它实际上成了配乐，随著音乐他登场了，这是一个非常让人振奋的时刻。

00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:12.420
and Jason leaned into it.
而且杰森完全融入其中。

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:14.039
I love that moment so much.
我太喜欢那个时刻了。

00:42:14.039 --> 00:42:18.260
And there's magic, like the fact that not Moby is at the same party they were gonna go to.
而且还有各种巧合，比如莫比（Moby）竟然也在他们原本要去的同一个派对上。

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:19.099
Right.
没错。

00:42:19.099 --> 00:42:21.699
The million, the 100 millionaire finding Robin at all.
那个身家百万、身家千万的富豪居然真的找到了罗宾。

00:42:21.699 --> 00:42:27.380
Marshall's name somehow magically calling him out of the mist, but his wife calling his name sort of makes him appear.
马修的名字不知怎么就像有魔力一样把他从迷雾中唤出，而他妻子呼唤他的名字似乎让他凭空出现。

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:30.619
I feel like he was way far away from them and a portal appeared.
我觉得他当时离他们超级远，然后出现了一个传送门。

00:42:30.619 --> 00:42:32.860
And you ran through the portal towards Lily's voice.
然后你穿过传送门朝莉莉的声音跑去。

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:35.659
And he jumped down three floors.
而且他从三层楼的高度跳了下来。

00:42:35.659 --> 00:42:38.940
Yeah, he jumped three floors, but Lily's voice made it all possible.
对，他从三楼跳下，但莉莉的声音让这一切成了可能。

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:39.940
He loves her so much.
他太爱她了。

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:41.619
I love that moment so much.
我太喜欢那个时刻了。

00:42:41.619 --> 00:42:57.500
What's wonderful about this shows use of that kind of magic is that it's almost leaning on the fact that you have purchased a kind of trust from your audience to then allow to break all the rules that you have.
这部剧运用这种魔力的妙处在于，它几乎是依托于这样一个事实：你已经赢得了观众的某种信任，然后你才可以打破你所设定的一切规则。

00:42:57.500 --> 00:43:06.380
Because certainly if you mess up continuity somewhere and you're wearing the wrong clothing, you're wearing the wrong guy, that's like a ding for an audience.
因为当然了，如果你在某处搞砸了连戏，穿错了衣服，找错了替身，观众是会挑剔的。

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:14.300
They'll sort of notice that if all of a sudden you just break reality and just say to say, what you're saying is, do you wanna have fun or not?
他们会注意到这些，如果突然之间你打破了现实，直接对观众说，你想不想找点乐子？

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:15.139
Right.
没错。

00:43:15.139 --> 00:43:22.860
And I think that once you've got them, they love these people and they know what the show is.
而且我觉得一旦你抓住了他们，他们就会爱上这些角色，并且他们知道这是一部什么样的剧。

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:25.820
Oh, it's not a show that's supernatural.
噢，这不是一部超自然的剧。

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:29.820
This is a real life show, I'm in.
这是一部现实生活的剧，我入坑了。

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:33.059
But now you're asking, hey, do you want to something crazy with me?
但现在你在要求：嘿，你想跟我一起搞点疯狂的吗？

00:43:33.059 --> 00:43:39.739
Come jump out a window with me and land three floors down And you had them at that point, and so you could introduce this kind of thing.
跟我一起跳出窗户，然后直接降落到三楼以下。而你当时已经把他们拿下了，所以你可以引入这种桥段。

00:43:39.739 --> 00:43:48.699
We trust the audience to make these magical realism-based leaves, except in the case of not being able to complete a cell phone call, in which case we have to say the circuit service.
我们信任观众会接受这种基于魔幻现实主义的跨越，除了手机信号断掉的那种情况，那种情况下我们必须解释是基站服务的问题。

00:43:48.699 --> 00:43:53.300
That one we had to explain, Marshall appearing out of the smoke, that's fine.
那个我们得解释一下，至于马修从烟雾中登场，那就没问题。

00:43:53.300 --> 00:44:03.980
Well, how we made Mother like, because it's this memory play, you're allowed to bend time in space and defy the laws of physics in a different way.
嗯，我们是怎么处理《老爸老妈浪漫史》的，因为这是一场回忆杀，你可以以不同的方式扭曲时空、违背物理定律。

00:44:03.980 --> 00:44:11.940
What a trick, what a magic trick to get to use, the trick of the unreliable narrator, the unreliability of memory itself.
这真是一个绝妙的招数，一个可以使用的魔术伎俩，那就是不靠谱的叙事者、记忆本身的不可靠性这一招。

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:19.619
This is another magic moment, is that Marshall steals back the CD from Not Moby.
这是另一个神奇的时刻，马修从“假莫比”手里把CD偷了回来。

00:44:20.019 --> 00:44:22.059
Like one, how would he know?
首先，他怎么会知道？

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:28.860
Like the amount of leaps that Marshall would have to make to figure out that this is Barney CD, Not Moby stole it.
马修得做多少脑回路跳跃，才能猜出这是巴尼的CD，被“假莫比”偷了。

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:29.699
How did you see that?
你怎么看出来的？

00:44:29.699 --> 00:44:31.500
But he thinks he's still Moby.
但他还以为对方真的是莫比。

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:42.349
But he steals it back, and it made me feel, because we get it, I don't remember when telepathy appeared, but it's one of my favorite how I made your mother jokes, is that they can speak telepathically to each other?
但他还是把它偷了回来，这让我觉得，因为我们都懂，我不记得心灵感应是什么时候出现的，但这绝对是我最喜欢的《老爸老妈浪漫史》的笑点之一，他们能用心灵感应互相交流吗？

00:44:42.349 --> 00:44:43.750
Yes, they speak telepathically.
对，他们用心灵感应交流。

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:47.909
But it made me realize that like, they actually already have telepathy.
但这让我意识到，他们其实已经拥有心灵感应了。

00:44:47.909 --> 00:44:48.750
Yes.
对。

00:44:48.750 --> 00:44:55.750
Like they know what they need, they anticipate each other's needs, they can, they're sensing something about each other.
就像他们知道彼此需要什么，他们能预判彼此的需求，他们能感应到对方身上的一些东西。

00:44:55.750 --> 00:44:59.230
They're an ecosystem or a biosphere, you know?
他们是一个生态系统或者生物圈，你知道吗？

00:44:59.230 --> 00:45:03.349
It's even better that he thought it was really Moby, He's like, I'm stealing that thing back from Moby.
更绝的是他以为对方真的是莫比，他当时想的是：我要从莫比手里把那东西偷回来。

00:45:03.349 --> 00:45:04.309
Moby cannot steal.
莫比不能偷东西。

00:45:04.309 --> 00:45:08.590
Moby may think he's a big, famous star, but he cannot steal my friends and get psyched mix.
莫比可能觉得自己是个大明星，但他绝对不能偷走我朋友的“振奋精选辑”。

00:45:08.590 --> 00:45:12.429
And he's saying, and Barney is like suddenly whole again when he gets that back.
而且他说，当巴尼拿回那张CD时，他突然又变得完整了。

00:45:12.429 --> 00:45:15.630
It's, yeah, I really loved rewatching this one.
就是，对，我真的很喜欢重温这一集。

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:16.909
I really, this is a fun one.
真的，这一集太有趣了。

00:45:16.909 --> 00:45:18.829
We didn't do a Christmas episode in season one.
我们在第一季里没有拍过圣诞节单集。

00:45:18.829 --> 00:45:19.989
I can't quite tell you why.
我其实说不上来为什么。

00:45:19.989 --> 00:45:21.429
Maybe it had to do with air dates.
也许跟播出日期有关。

00:45:21.429 --> 00:45:23.150
Maybe we just didn't have a good idea for it.
也许我们当时只是没有好的点子。

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:29.510
We had a good idea for Thanksgiving episode and a good idea for a New Year's episode and a good idea for a Halloween episode, but we skipped Christmas.
我们想出了感恩节单集的绝妙点子，跨年单集的绝妙点子，万圣节单集的绝妙点子，但我们唯独跳过了圣诞节。

00:45:29.510 --> 00:45:31.309
I'm so glad we did a New Year's one.
我很高兴我们拍了一集跨年单集。

00:45:31.309 --> 00:45:40.389
What I loved about the comment that began this episode, I forget her name, forgive me, I like to remember her person's name at the beginning, Andrea, from Remind me of everything about Andrea, from Mexico.
我最喜欢本集开头那个评论的一点是——我忘了她的名字，请原谅我，我其实很想记住开头那个人的名字——安德烈（Andrea），来自……提醒我一下关于来自墨西哥的安德烈的一切。

00:45:40.389 --> 00:45:43.949
I just wanted to share what I was gonna write.
我只是想分享一下我本来打算写的内容。

00:45:43.949 --> 00:45:48.590
What I loved about her comment was that she says, she watches it every year right after midnight.
我最喜欢她的评论的地方在于，她说她每年午夜过后都会准时看这一集。

00:45:48.590 --> 00:45:51.030
I love that that's part of her tradition, you know what I mean?
我好喜欢这成了她的传统，你知道我的意思吗？

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:54.909
That's when she hits play on that, just like starting up the Bon Jovi song.
这就是她按下播放键的时刻，就像播放邦·乔维的那首歌一样。

00:45:54.909 --> 00:45:57.150
So thank you, Andrea, sorry, I forgot your name briefly.
所以谢谢你，安德烈，抱歉我刚才一下子忘了你的名字。

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:58.789
For Mexico, your comment was amazing.
来自墨西哥的你，你的评论太棒了。

00:45:58.789 --> 00:46:01.510
I love that it's part of your New Year's tradition.
我很高兴这成了你新年传统的一部分。

00:46:01.510 --> 00:46:05.510
We didn't do too many New Year's episodes, but this one was a good one.
我们没拍过太多跨年单集，但这一集确实很棒。

00:46:05.510 --> 00:46:13.590
I feel like we're actually up to the point plot-wise where Derek comes back and Ted is crushed.
我觉得我们在剧情上已经推进到了德里克（Derek）回来、而特德（Ted）心碎的时刻。

00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:24.420
And I will say, I don't know, there was something, I really believed Ted's devastation when Derek hops in that limo.
我得说，我不知道，有那么一瞬间，当德里克跳进加长轿车时，我是真的相信了特德那崩溃的心情。

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:29.619
Like there was something about, there was something about my face that I was like, That was good, that was good film acting, Radner.
就好像我的脸上有一种表情，让我觉得：演得好，这电影演技真棒，雷德纳（Radner）。

00:46:29.619 --> 00:46:31.320
I'll say, I like hearing you say that.
我得说，我很高兴听你这么说。

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:34.320
I like hearing you admit that you've been good on this TV show.
我很高兴听到你自己承认在这部电视剧里表现得很出色。

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:36.380
I felt like, I really felt for him.
我当时真的对他的遭遇感同身受。

00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:40.099
I was like, oh, this is such a bummer for him.
我心想，天哪，这对他是多么沉重的打击啊。

00:46:40.099 --> 00:46:45.460
And the night was going particularly well, you know, for him, vis-a-vis Robin up until that point.
而且在那之前，今晚他和罗宾的进展其实特别顺利，你知道的。

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:52.659
But I think he says the line, or maybe the narrator says, all night long, I've been trying to chase something down that was right there in that limo.
但我记得他说过那句台词，或者可能是旁白说的：“整晚我都一直在试图追逐某个东西，而它其实就在那辆豪华轿车里。”

00:46:52.659 --> 00:46:53.500
Does he say that?
他是这么说的吗？

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:54.659
Yeah, it says.
对，剧里是这么说的。

00:46:54.659 --> 00:47:01.619
And it made me think about, you know, you really watch these people from their mid to late 20s to their early to mid 30s.
这让我想到，你看，这些人从20多岁的中后期一直到30多岁的前中期。

00:47:01.619 --> 00:47:03.699
That's kind of the arc.
这差不多就是故事的发展轨迹。

00:47:03.699 --> 00:47:10.219
And it's a really pivotal time, that decade, 25 to 35, let's call it, is huge, right?
这是一个非常关键的时期，25到35岁这十年，可以说是至关重要的，对吧？

00:47:10.219 --> 00:47:11.619
And there's a huge transition.
而且会经历巨大的转变。

00:47:11.619 --> 00:47:16.260
And I think your 20s are really about chasing experiences.
我认为20多岁的时候，其实就是在追逐各种体验。

00:47:16.260 --> 00:47:18.860
That's why it's like, we have five parties to hit.
这就是为什么我们会想：“我们要去赶五个派对。”

00:47:18.860 --> 00:47:29.931
We're going out, we're gonna like go West, young man, like we're seeking our fortune, we're seeking romance, seeking connection, you know, all of it, and it's out there, right?
我们要出去玩，我们要像“年轻人，去西部吧”那样，去寻找财富，寻找浪漫，寻找连结，你知道，所有这些，而且它们就在外面，对吧？

00:47:29.931 --> 00:47:31.755
Everything is outside of you.
一切都在你的外部。

00:47:31.755 --> 00:47:39.303
But then maturity, I wrote this down, is realizing it's about what's inside you and what's right in front of you, right?
但随后，成熟——我把这个记下来了——就是意识到这一切其实关乎你的内心以及你眼前的人，对吧？

00:47:39.303 --> 00:47:43.015
Like it's like there's not a better life lurking out there.
就好像外面并没有潜藏着什么更好的生活。

00:47:43.015 --> 00:47:48.173
It's like the universe is inside me and also the people I love are in front of me.
这就好比宇宙就在我心中，同时我爱的人就在我眼前。

00:47:48.173 --> 00:47:51.066
And it's best to be present with those people.
最好还是和这些人活在当下。

00:47:51.066 --> 00:48:04.779
So I think that in some ways that's the moral of the episode, which is like, and Ted changes Ted, Ted learns that in the episode because he's driving them all over the city and he says, no, the best parties right here.
所以我觉得从某种程度上来说，这就是这一集的内核，也就是——泰德也因此改变了，泰德在这集里学到了这一点，因为他开车带着大家跑遍了整个城市，然后他说：“不，最好的派对就在这里。”

00:48:04.780 --> 00:48:07.025
I loved Ted in that moment when he said that.
我特别喜欢泰德说这句话时的那一刻。

00:48:07.025 --> 00:48:14.659
It is it's this is this is as clear an illustration of it's about the journey, not the destination because they literally never get to their destination.
确实如此，这简直是对“过程比终点更重要”最清晰不过的诠释了，因为他们实际上根本就没有到达终点。

00:48:14.659 --> 00:48:16.423
You never get out of the limo.
你们从来没有下过那辆豪华轿车。

00:48:16.423 --> 00:48:17.422
It's all journey.
全都是过程。

00:48:17.422 --> 00:48:18.774
Yeah, it's all journey.
对，全都是过程。

00:48:18.774 --> 00:48:22.006
But not the journey song, except for the not that song.
但不是那首《Journey》的歌，除开那首歌不算。

00:48:22.006 --> 00:48:23.123
Sorry for the Lord.
抱歉啊，Lord。

00:48:23.123 --> 00:48:26.590
And to be fair, it also would have worked great to be fair.
公平地说，其实用那首歌效果也会很棒的。

00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:38.639
No, no, I could, I could, you could make a case for that, but there's something totally because shot that the shot through the heart really just works every time you just pop so hard comes in at a million.
不，不，我可以说，你可以为那首歌找理由，但确实有些东西——因为邦·乔维的《Shot Through the Heart》每一次都真的太管用了，一出来情绪直接拉满到顶点。

00:48:38.639 --> 00:48:43.164
I will say that I thought the last scene with me and Kobe was pretty perfect.
我得说，我觉得我和寇比的最后一场戏相当完美。

00:48:43.164 --> 00:48:44.928
The writing of it was perfect.
它的剧本写得太完美了。

00:48:44.928 --> 00:48:46.279
There was no fat on it.
没有一丝多余的废话。

00:48:46.279 --> 00:48:47.455
It's just so simple.
就是这么简单。

00:48:47.455 --> 00:48:53.567
And you have enough history fueling them at this point, 11 episodes in, where not much needs to be said.
而且此时此刻，在播了11集之后，你们之间已经有了足够的情感积淀，不需要说太多话。

00:48:53.567 --> 00:48:58.093
It's like, it's this, you know, and you could almost plausibly make the case.
感觉就像是，就是这样，你知道的，你几乎可以合情合理地得出这样的解读。

00:48:58.093 --> 00:49:01.384
Let's say Ted thought it was a bigger deal than she did.
比方说，泰德觉得这件事比她认为的更重要。

00:49:01.384 --> 00:49:06.145
You could almost make the case like, you could interpret that as romantic or not.
你几乎可以这样解读：你可以把这看作是浪漫的，也可以不这么看。

00:49:06.145 --> 00:49:09.789
Like it was actually, we threaded an interesting needle there.
就好像实际上，我们在那里巧妙地平衡了一个微妙的平衡点。

00:49:09.789 --> 00:49:11.140
Yeah, it was enigmatic.
对，这很神秘。

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:13.139
There was something charged to it.
里面有一种情绪在暗流涌动。

00:49:13.139 --> 00:49:13.903
It was there.
那种感觉是存在的。

00:49:13.903 --> 00:49:15.020
I love that moment.
我太喜欢那个时刻了。

00:49:15.019 --> 00:49:19.860
And it tees up the entire rest of season one in the best possible way.
它以最好的方式为整个第一季的后续内容做好了铺垫。

00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:31.139
Pineapple kind of brought it back, sort of got those embers going again a little bit with Ted's recalling her and bringing this back up at him and a little bit on the back burner, the Robin thing for a while, and this is bringing it back forward and this launches.
《凤梨案》差不多把这个话题又拉了回来，通过泰德回忆她并再次提起这件事，让那些火星重新燃起了一点，而罗宾这条线之前稍微被搁置了一段时间，现在又把它带回了前方，从而拉开了序幕。

00:49:31.139 --> 00:49:36.500
I would argue this is a really good midway point of a season episode because it really launches the second half.
我认为这是整季中一个非常出色的中点集，因为它真正拉开了下半季的序幕。

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:37.500
Yeah.
对。

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:38.500
Yeah.
对。

00:49:38.500 --> 00:49:44.260
It also made me feel like when I was watching Ted really through the first 11 episodes, like I think there's a pattern.
这也让我觉得，当我看着泰德在前11集里的表现时，我觉得这里面有一种模式。

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:53.019
I don't know if this is like a, if there's a term for this, but I hear from a lot of people who say, oh, I was very dear friends with my husband.
我不知道这算不算，或者有没有专门的术语，但我经常听到很多人说：“哦，我和我丈夫以前是非常要好的朋友。”

00:49:53.019 --> 00:49:56.460
He was just in my friend group, and I never thought of him that way.
“他只是我朋友圈里的一员，我从来没那样想过他。”

00:49:56.460 --> 00:50:06.639
And then you kind of like, you know, when the lights come up at the end of whatever your age, and you go, oh, this is the person I like the most and feel the most comfortable around.
然后差不多就像是，你知道的，当不管你到了什么年纪，在最后时刻灯光亮起时，你会想：“哦，这就是我最喜欢、在一起最觉得舒服的人。”

00:50:06.639 --> 00:50:16.085
I feel like Ted's kind of like the prototype for the guy like in your friend group that a woman eventually realizes is like the best kindest guy and falls for him.
我觉得泰德有点像那种原型：就是你朋友圈里的那种男生，女人最终会意识到他是最好、最善良的家伙，然后爱上他。

00:50:16.085 --> 00:50:21.532
But he's easy to overlook when you're on that experience hunt, you kind of don't see that guy.
但当你处于那种体验的追逐中时，他很容易被忽视，你有点注意不到那个男生。

00:50:21.532 --> 00:50:30.340
And I think that's what he said in his first monologue, that monologue in the pilot, like, I'm not good at this stuff, but I'd be a really good husband.
我想这也就是他在第一集的开场独白里所说的，试播集里的那段独白：我不擅长这些东西，但我会是一个非常好的丈夫。

00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:35.059
Once you realize it's not about getting to the five parties, the parties right here.
一旦你意识到重点根本不是去赶那五个派对，派对就在这里。

00:50:36.099 --> 00:50:38.070
This is it, the party is here.
就是这样，派对就在这里。

00:50:38.070 --> 00:51:07.500
And I think they get so excited when Marshall comes back, or lily gets back because it's like it doesn't feel right unless they're all together and in some ways this episode more than any other episode is the five of them are a gang like they're an absolute gang and Robin has been fully folded in so yeah I was actually I was pretty delighted by this episode I remember loving the script and and it being a hard shoot but I think it It's worth it.
而且我觉得当马歇尔回来时，或者莉莉回来时，他们会那么兴奋，因为感觉如果不是大家聚在一起，一切就不对劲。从某种程度上来说，这一集比其他任何一集都更能体现他们五个人是一个小团体，他们绝对是一个铁打的团队，而且罗宾也已经完全融入进来了，所以，是的，我对这一集其实感到非常高兴。我记得我很喜欢这个剧本，虽然拍摄过程很艰难，但我认为这一切都是值得的。

00:51:07.500 --> 00:51:08.500
It was worth it.
这是值得的。

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:09.500
Yeah.
对。

00:51:09.500 --> 00:51:15.260
We thought we were giving everyone the simplest shoot of the year and everyone was cursing our names by the end of the week, but it was a good one.
我们本以为我们会给每个人安排全年最轻松的一次拍摄，结果到周末结束时，每个人都在诅咒我们的名字，但这是一次很棒的拍摄。

00:51:15.260 --> 00:51:29.619
So we're up to our very special section, which I think I'm going to amend this, Alec, but I'm just now going to call this questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
所以，我们来到了非常特别的环节，我想我要修改一下这个称呼，亚历克，我以后要把它叫做：“一个从未看过《老爸老妈的浪漫史》、恰好又嫁给乔斯的临床心理学家的疑问与观察”。

00:51:29.619 --> 00:51:32.460
I just cut out relationship expert because I don't know.
我刚刚把“恋爱专家”这个词删掉了，因为我不知道。

00:51:32.460 --> 00:51:39.561
I don't know that she would describe herself that way and I don't know that anyone could describe themselves that way.
我不知道她会怎么形容自己，我也不知道任何人会这样形容自己。

00:51:39.561 --> 00:51:40.825
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:51:40.825 --> 00:51:48.469
I'd be wary of a relationship expert, except Terry Reel, who's a very interesting psychologist, if you've ever listened to him.
我对所谓的恋爱专家持谨慎态度，特里·里尔除外，他是一位非常有趣的心理学家，如果你听过他的讲座的话。

00:51:48.469 --> 00:51:50.876
But he's very smart about relationships.
但他对亲密关系的理解确实非常聪明。

00:51:50.876 --> 00:51:52.622
But anyway, we will amend it.
不过没关系，我们会修改的。

00:51:52.622 --> 00:52:01.228
We will have amended it in our graphic as you are if you're watching this on YouTube, you removed and here she is, the not relationship expert.
我们会在视频画面里修改好，如果你是在YouTube上观看的话，你已经看不到“恋爱专家”这几个字了，而她就在这里。

00:52:01.228 --> 00:52:11.459
This episode almost felt like a microcosm of a macro theme of the series, which I think is summed up by what Ted said at the very end of the episode, and I wrote it down.
这一集几乎就像是整部剧宏大主题的一个微缩版，我觉得泰德在剧集最后所说的话很好地总结了这一点，我都把它记下来了。

00:52:11.460 --> 00:52:16.820
So he said, you probably want to feel bad for your old man at this point in the story.
他是这么说的：在这个故事的这个阶段，你可能想为你们的老头子感到难过。

00:52:17.380 --> 00:52:18.243
Well, don't.
好吧，大可不必。

00:52:18.243 --> 00:52:24.936
Not every night has a happy ending, but all of it's important, all of it's leading somewhere.
并不是每个夜晚都有一个幸福的结局，但所有这一切都很重要，所有这一切都在通往某个地方。

00:52:24.936 --> 00:52:29.399
And for sitcom, this is truly a metaphysical spiritual lesson.
对于一部情景喜剧来说，这真的是一堂形而上学的精神课。

00:52:29.399 --> 00:52:32.134
We make plans, the unexpected happens.
我们制定计划，意料之外的事情就会发生。

00:52:32.134 --> 00:52:38.899
We have a small degree of control and then there's a massive amount that's beyond our control.
我们只有一小部分是可以控制的，而有很大一部分是我们无法控制的。

00:52:38.899 --> 00:52:47.607
In this case, the traffic, Lily's feet hurting, Marshall forgetting his phone, not Moby, waving around a gun in the limo.
在这个例子中，就是交通堵塞、莉莉脚疼、马歇尔忘带手机，还有莫比（Moby）没在豪华轿车里挥舞枪支。

00:52:47.607 --> 00:52:59.339
But the message of the episode that I took home was to have faith that whatever happens, even if it seems absolutely awful at the time, it is indeed all important.
但我从这一集里领悟到的讯息是：要怀有信念，无论发生什么，即使当时看起来绝对糟糕透顶，但这一切确实都至关重要。

00:52:59.339 --> 00:53:04.377
It's all leading you somewhere and that somewhere is where you belong.
这一切都在指引你前往某个地方，而那个地方正是你属于的地方。

00:53:04.377 --> 00:53:12.581
And for Ted, the planner whose plans were undone, it still ultimately led to that moment that we were waiting for.
而对于泰德这个计划被打乱的规划者来说，它最终还是通往了我们一直在等待的那个时刻。

00:53:12.581 --> 00:53:14.021
That kiss was Robin.
那个吻就是罗宾。

00:53:14.021 --> 00:53:26.760
And though this is only episode 11 and I have just shy of 200 more to go, my best guess is the entire show is going on fold following the spiritual lesson in a very similar way.
尽管这只是第11集，我还有将近200集要看，但我最好的猜测是，整部剧都将以非常相似的方式遵循这堂精神课展开。

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:27.760
Wow.
哇。

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:30.699
That's, but that's too good.
这，但这太棒了。

00:53:30.699 --> 00:53:32.820
That's better than anything we've said so far.
这比我们之前说的任何话都要好。

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:34.820
Can we just, Alex, reset it down to that one?
亚历克，我们能不能干脆把重心重新锁定到这一段上？

00:53:34.820 --> 00:53:37.420
It's just going to be Andrea and Jordana.
干脆就变成安德莉亚和乔丹娜得了。

00:53:37.420 --> 00:53:38.420
Yeah.
对。

00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:39.420
Yeah.
对。

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:40.420
I think that's a good cut.
我觉得这是一个很好的剪辑。

00:53:40.420 --> 00:53:41.420
Cut out, everybody.
把大家都剪掉。

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:43.219
Cut out all the dudes.
把所有男士都剪掉。

00:53:43.219 --> 00:53:44.219
That's such a great comment.
这真是一个绝妙的评论。

00:53:44.219 --> 00:53:51.059
I love that she singled out that ending narration and I love that idea of it's all important, which is different than saying it's all going to work out.
我很高兴她特意指出了最后的旁白，我也很喜欢“这一切都很重要”这个观点，这和说“一切都会圆满解决”是不一样的。

00:53:51.059 --> 00:53:55.619
I'm happy with that wording and that narration because it's true, it's all, it's important.
我对这种措辞和那段旁白很满意，因为这是真的，一切，都很重要。

00:53:55.619 --> 00:53:56.980
It's not all good.
并不全是好事。

00:53:56.980 --> 00:53:57.980
It's not all bad.
也并不全是坏事。

00:53:57.980 --> 00:53:58.980
It's important.
这一切都很重要。

00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:10.820
It's important because it's part of your story and it's the accumulation of those sort of story beats of your own life that lead you where you need to be or where you want to be or where you hope to be or where you never thought you would be, but then you're happy there.
它之所以重要，是因为它是你故事的一部分，正是你生活中那些故事片段的积累，把你带到了你需要去的地方，或者你想去的地方，或者你希望去的地方，或者你从未想过自己会去、但去了之后却感到幸福的地方。

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:22.260
You know, it reminded me of something I'd read that said, like, never trust a memoir or a biography that doesn't have like some scandal in it or the protagonist doesn't look bad.
你知道，这让我想起我读过的一句话，上面说：永远不要相信一本没有包含任何丑闻或者没有让主角显得有些糟糕的回忆录或传记。

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:23.260
Yeah.
对。

00:54:23.260 --> 00:54:26.619
any biography where it's just, that's hagiography, right?
任何那种把人物写得完美无缺的传记，那叫圣徒传记，对吧？

00:54:26.619 --> 00:54:29.980
Where is that like how they describe like the lives of saints?
就像他们描述圣徒生平的那种？

00:54:29.980 --> 00:54:34.570
Like no one's that interested in reading how it all went great.
没有人会对阅读“一切都顺风顺水”有多大兴趣。

00:54:34.570 --> 00:54:44.170
Like we want to hear struggle, we want to, and you trust a narrator when they, actually Jason's really great at telling stories where he's kind of the butt of the joke.
我们想听到的是挣扎，我们想——而且当一个旁白者能够讲述那些让自己成为笑柄的故事时，你是会信任他的，其实杰森就很擅长讲这种故事。

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:47.050
Like he loves it when he has been humiliated.
就像他很享受自己出丑的时候。

00:54:47.050 --> 00:54:48.409
Like he loves telling those stories.
就像他很喜欢讲那些故事。

00:54:48.409 --> 00:54:49.769
I always admired that about him.
我一直很欣赏他这一点。

00:54:49.769 --> 00:54:55.130
You know, he's really good at telling stories where it's like he doesn't look that great in the story.
你知道，他特别擅长讲那种他在故事里显得不那么光彩的故事。

00:54:55.130 --> 00:55:01.809
But him telling it is charming and you forgive him whatever his behavior was because he's willing to share it.
但由于他愿意分享，他讲起来很有魅力，你也会原谅他当时的行为。

00:55:01.809 --> 00:55:20.000
It's almost like one of the things, this is going deep in the weeds, but one of the reasons that the Torah, the Old Testament, was a revolutionary kind of earthquake and narrative is because every story of a people was written by the victors.
这简直就像是——有点扯远了——但为什么《托拉》（即《旧约》）会成为一种具有革命性的、颠覆性的叙事的原因之一，是因为每个民族的历史都是由胜利者书写的。

00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:22.679
And it was written about how great everyone was.
而且书写的内容都是关于每个人有多么伟大。

00:55:22.679 --> 00:55:32.059
And the Torah and the story of this little tribe that got knocked around is just one idiotic move after another.
而《托拉》以及这个饱受欺凌的小部落的故事，却记录了一个又一个愚蠢的举动。

00:55:32.059 --> 00:55:36.119
Like they really cataloged their own folly, right?
就像他们真的把自己的愚蠢都归纳进去了，对吧？

00:55:36.119 --> 00:55:41.019
And their own, and how they didn't listen to the law or this way and they didn't, you know?
还有他们自己的，他们是如何不遵守律法之类的，以及他们没有，你知道吗？

00:55:41.019 --> 00:55:48.699
And it's maybe it's endured because it's honest and it's not hagiography.
也许它之所以能流传下来，是因为它很诚实，而不是圣徒传。

00:55:48.699 --> 00:55:53.699
It's, I think we trust people, we're not here.
我觉得我们信任别人，我们不在这里……

00:55:54.300 --> 00:55:58.719
When you list your accomplishments, you breed competition.
当你列举你的成就时，你滋生的是竞争。

00:55:58.719 --> 00:56:02.500
When you lead with vulnerability, you breed community.
当你以脆弱引领时，你建立的是社群。

00:56:02.500 --> 00:56:03.247
You know?
你知道吗？

00:56:03.247 --> 00:56:03.747
Yes.
是的。

00:56:03.579 --> 00:56:09.139
So I think, you know, even though Ted, sometimes you can go like, oh God, why won't this guy get it together?
所以我觉得，尽管泰德有时会让你觉得，天哪，这家伙怎么就不能振作起来呢？

00:56:09.139 --> 00:56:14.539
It's actually his superpower that he is leading with, here's another way I messed up.
但实际上，以“这是我又一次搞砸了”来开诚布公，正是他的超能力。

00:56:14.539 --> 00:56:15.900
Oh my God, let me tell you about this night.
哦天哪，让我跟你讲讲那天晚上的事。

00:56:15.900 --> 00:56:17.780
I really put my foot in my mouth.
我真是说错话了。

00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:18.619
Let me tell you this.
我来跟你讲讲这个。

00:56:18.619 --> 00:56:23.300
The embodiment of this is all getting dinged up.
这种形象的化身就是到处受挫。

00:56:23.300 --> 00:56:31.500
And in some ways, that's actually, it's occurring to me now, it's an incredibly loving thing to tell your children when they're ready to hear.
从某种程度上来说，我现在突然意识到，当你的孩子准备好倾听时，对他们说这样的话是一件极其充满爱意的事。

00:56:31.500 --> 00:56:34.539
I'm an imperfect human being, your dad just grew up.
我是一个不完美的人，你爸爸也只是刚刚长大。

00:56:34.539 --> 00:56:37.940
But also, he landed on his feet and he did okay.
但同时，他挺过来了，而且做得还不错。

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:46.420
So I think it's a great observation that there's a kind of microcosm, macrocosm thing going on here that Jordana pointed out.
所以我觉得这是一个很棒的观察，正如乔丹娜所指出的，这里面存在某种微观与宏观的呼应。

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:47.639
Yeah, it's a great observation.
对，这是一个很棒的观察。

00:56:47.639 --> 00:56:48.880
Isn't my wife smart?
我老婆是不是很聪明？

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:50.000
Your wife's really smart.
你老婆真的很聪明。

00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:50.500
Yeah.
对。

00:56:50.500 --> 00:56:51.345
She's really good.
她真的很棒。

00:56:51.079 --> 00:56:52.119
You should hold on to that one.
你可得好好珍惜这个。

00:56:52.119 --> 00:56:56.119
Whether or not she's officially a relationship expert, I think she used to keep her around.
不管她是不是官方认证的情感专家，我觉得她以前应该留着她。

00:56:56.119 --> 00:56:57.719
I think it's like she may be a realtor.
我觉得她可能是个房地产经纪人。

00:56:57.719 --> 00:57:00.239
I think she's really not she's officially your wife.
我觉得她其实不是……她官方身份是你老婆。

00:57:00.239 --> 00:57:02.320
And I was like, yeah, she's officially my wife.
然后我说，对啊，她官方身份就是我老婆。

00:57:02.320 --> 00:57:03.320
Back off, man.
离远点，老兄。

00:57:08.780 --> 00:57:12.659
Well, that was super fun, Craig, going deep on that episode.
克雷格，深入探讨那一集节目真的超级有趣。

00:57:12.659 --> 00:57:13.579
I really enjoyed that.
我真的很享受。

00:57:13.579 --> 00:57:14.500
I liked that one.
我挺喜欢那一集的。

00:57:14.500 --> 00:57:15.340
Yeah.
是啊。

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:16.500
It's a good one.
那一集很不错。

00:57:16.500 --> 00:57:24.340
Isn't it funny that we're gonna have favorite episodes of this show that won't necessarily correspond to our favorite episodes of How I Met Your Mother.
你不觉得很有趣吗？我们对这个节目的最爱剧集，未必会和我们对《老爸老妈浪漫史》的最爱剧集相对应。

00:57:24.340 --> 00:57:27.539
They're just like the conversation, it led somewhere cool, right?
它们只是那种对话，聊到了一些很酷的地方，对吧？

00:57:27.539 --> 00:57:28.380
Like it'll be.
就像会是这样。

00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:31.539
The Hemium episode was garbage, but this podcast episode was really fun.
那一集《老爸老妈浪漫史》虽然是垃圾，但这一期播客节目真的很有趣。

00:57:31.539 --> 00:57:32.820
The circuits were jammed though.
不过线路堵塞了。

00:57:32.820 --> 00:57:34.539
The circuits were jammed.
线路堵塞了。

00:57:34.539 --> 00:57:37.099
If we can't get anything going, like we're just like the circuits are jammed.
如果我们什么都进展不顺利，就像我们在说“线路堵塞了”一样。

00:57:37.099 --> 00:57:38.420
The circuits are jammed today.
今天线路堵塞了。

00:57:38.420 --> 00:57:40.380
Look, that's New Year's Eve for ya.
听着，这就是跨年夜。

00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:44.099
You're welcome for that turn of phrase.
不客气，算我妙语连珠。

00:57:44.099 --> 00:57:50.780
Well, we like to end our episode with some viewer listener mail.
好了，我们喜欢用观众和听众来信来结束这一集。

00:57:50.780 --> 00:57:58.239
And we have a lovely letter today from Jessica and Craig is going to read it for us.
今天我们收到了一封可爱的信，杰西卡写的，克雷格将为我们朗读。

00:57:58.239 --> 00:57:59.480
Yes, this is lovely.
好的，这封信真温馨。

00:57:59.480 --> 00:58:04.480
And a little conversation talking point emerges at the end of it.
而且信的结尾还引出了一个小小的讨论话题。

00:58:04.480 --> 00:58:05.320
So here we go, ready?
那么我们开始吧，准备好了吗？

00:58:05.320 --> 00:58:06.320
This is Jessica.
发信人是杰西卡。

00:58:06.320 --> 00:58:07.960
She says, hi, Josh and Craig.
她说，嗨，乔希和克雷格。

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:09.760
Jessica here from New Brunswick, Canada.
我是来自加拿大新不伦瑞克的杰西卡。

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:10.880
Shout out to Canada.
向加拿大问好。

00:58:10.880 --> 00:58:12.679
We love our Canadian fans.
我们爱我们的加拿大粉丝。

00:58:12.679 --> 00:58:15.639
And we love our Canadian characters as a plural.
还有我们复数的加拿大角色们。

00:58:15.639 --> 00:58:18.780
I think we had a few over the years, mainly Robin.
我想这些年我们有过好几个，主要是罗宾。

00:58:18.780 --> 00:58:25.380
My husband Tristan introduced me to him when we first started dating and it quickly became a cherished part of our lives.
我丈夫特里斯坦在我们刚开始约会时把我引荐给这部剧，它很快成为了我们生活中不可或缺的一部分。

00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:29.900
The show holds such significance to us that it actually played a role in our wedding day.
这部剧对我们意义重大，甚至在我们的婚礼上扮演了角色。

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:39.539
From gifting my soon to be husband of Blue French Horn to open the morning of the wedding, to having Heaven by the Walkman play during our recessional, great song.
从在婚礼早晨送给我未婚夫一把蓝色法国号作为礼物，到在我们的退场环节播放《The Walkman》乐队的《Heaven》（一首好歌）。

00:58:39.539 --> 00:58:41.619
You have to wait till the end of the series, you get to it.
你得等到剧集大结局才能看到那段。

00:58:41.619 --> 00:58:48.079
And the unforgettable moment of our bosses surprising us with a cameo of Josh during their heartfelt speech.
还有那个难忘的时刻，我们的老板在他们深情的演讲中，用乔希的客串视频给了我们一个惊喜。

00:58:48.079 --> 00:59:02.639
I know cameos might seem like a small gesture of celebrities, but hearing Josh say, as Ted Mosby said, love is the best thing we do on our wedding day, was nothing short of legendary captured beautifully in the photos and video footage of our reactions.
我知道对名人们来说，客串可能只是一个小小的举动，但听到乔希说出“正如泰德·莫斯比所说，爱是我们做过最好的事”在我们的婚礼当天简直堪称传奇，从我们反应的照片和视频中可以完美捕捉到那份感动。

00:59:02.639 --> 00:59:05.960
Please send us those, by the way, Jessica.
顺便说一句，杰西卡，请把那些发给我们看看。

00:59:05.960 --> 00:59:13.760
It warms my heart to see how both of you remain humbled and perhaps a touch astonished by the profound impact your creation has had on countless lives.
看到你们两个对你们的创作对无数人生产生的深远影响仍然保持谦逊，甚至可能还有一丝惊讶，这让我感到心里暖洋洋的。

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:21.920
My question for both of you is this, is there a show or film series that evokes the same feelings in you that Hemium does for its fans?
我给两人的问题是：有没有哪部剧或系列电影，能唤起你们那种与粉丝对本剧产生的一模一样的情感？

00:59:21.920 --> 00:59:24.840
You're going to anticipate this journey of dissecting every last episode.
你们即将开启剖析每一集的心路历程。

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:33.059
Please know that you both, and Carter, have conjured something truly spectacular, a treasure that will continue to resonate for years to come.
请知道，你们俩以及卡特，已经创造了一些真正壮观的东西，这是一笔将继续产生共鸣的宝贵财富。

00:59:33.059 --> 00:59:34.059
Thank you, Jessica.
谢谢你，杰西卡。

00:59:34.059 --> 00:59:34.900
Well, thanks, Jessica.
好的，谢谢你，杰西卡。

00:59:34.900 --> 00:59:38.340
What a lovely, every part of that, being part of their wedding.
多么可爱啊，信的每一个部分，成为他们婚礼的一部分。

00:59:38.340 --> 00:59:40.019
I love that you did that cameo.
我很高兴你做了那个客串。

00:59:40.019 --> 00:59:46.692
I don't know if you remember doing that specific one years ago or not, did you know what was going to be part of a wedding?
我不知道你是否还记得几年前做过那个特定的客串，你当时知道那是婚礼的一部分吗？

00:59:46.692 --> 00:59:51.520
I don't, I'm, I mean, I mean, I had asked, I always make a joke about being underdressed.
我不记得了，我的意思是，我的意思是，我曾要求过，我总是开玩笑说自己穿得不够正式。

00:59:51.520 --> 00:59:52.659
That's kind of money.
那倒也挺符合身份的。

00:59:53.860 --> 00:59:58.756
But yeah, I mean, I sometimes they'll say, can you throw in a Ted Mosby quote, you love.
但对，我的意思是，有时他们会说，你能加入一句你喜欢的泰德·莫斯比台词吗？

00:59:58.756 --> 01:00:03.875
And for me, that's like the one, you know, from, for a wedding, I think that's the best one.
对我来说，这就好像是那句，你知道的，为了婚礼，我认为那是最好的一句。

01:00:03.875 --> 01:00:04.375
Yeah.
对。

01:00:04.375 --> 01:00:06.322
Yeah, it's the whole series, right?
是的，这是整个系列的精髓，对吧？

01:00:06.322 --> 01:00:12.721
That's that if you had a distilled series down to one line, that could, that could be in the running to be the one.
如果你把整部剧浓缩成一句台词，那句台词绝对有竞争力的候选资格。

01:00:12.500 --> 01:00:16.019
And I think it's, you know, there's different kinds of love.
而且我认为，你知道，爱有很多种。

01:00:16.019 --> 01:00:17.754
Like we have that one word, but like C.S.
就像我们只有一个“爱”字，但比如C.S.

01:00:17.754 --> 01:00:19.699
Lewis has a book called The Four Loves, right?
刘易斯有一本书叫《四种爱》，对吧？

01:00:19.699 --> 01:00:30.659
It's like Eros and Philos, and like there's different, but I think love more broadly speaking is kind of how I'm able to, because there's certainly friendship, love.
就像厄洛斯（浪漫之爱）和菲利亚（手足之爱），有不同的爱，但我觉得更广泛意义上的爱，就是我能理解的那种，因为里面肯定有友情之爱。

01:00:30.659 --> 01:00:32.585
It's so much about friendship.
这很大程度上关于友情。

01:00:32.585 --> 01:00:34.126
It's mostly that, right?
基本上都是这个，对吧？

01:00:34.126 --> 01:00:39.005
In the end, it's a lot of of dates but the friendships there the whole time.
最后，虽然有很多约会，但友情自始至终都在。

01:00:39.005 --> 01:00:46.260
Do you have pieces of art that live and take residence in your heart the way that him, him, has heard these fans?
你们有没有哪些艺术作品，像这部剧长驻在这些粉丝心中那样，也在你们的心中占据一席之地并长久居住？

01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:59.431
I mean, for me, I feel like what Jessica's talking about and I think the feeling she's talking about can only be achieved through a franchise, a series, something that goes on a while that you can spend that much time with.
对我来说，我觉得杰西卡所说的，以及她所说的那种感觉，只有通过一个系列、一部剧集、一些持续了一段时间、你能花那么多时间陪伴的东西才能实现。

01:00:59.431 --> 01:01:03.980
Your favorite movie that's like an hour hour and a half long, two hours long.
你最喜欢的电影大概一个小时、一个半小时到两个小时长。

01:01:03.980 --> 01:01:07.500
You can go back into that world over and over, but it's kind of always that one thing.
你可以一次又一次地回到那个世界，但它总是那样单一的东西。

01:01:07.500 --> 01:01:14.219
It's hard to replicate that, but what she's talking about, what a TV series can give you, it's such a gift to get nine seasons to do a show.
这很难复制，但她所说的，电视剧能带给你的东西，能拍九季的剧集简直是一份天大的礼物。

01:01:14.219 --> 01:01:24.860
So for me, what comes up for me when she says that, is like, Star Wars, the Star Wars franchise, just getting to, that stretching across my entire childhood into my adult life.
所以对我来说，当她这么说时，我想到了星球大战，星战系列，它贯穿了我的整个童年一直到成年生活。

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:29.619
The Harry Potter books and movies, give me some of that feeling.
《哈利·波特》的书和电影，就给了我那种感觉。

01:01:29.619 --> 01:01:54.119
And then sitcom wise, you know, what made me want to write sitcoms was was cheers mostly to show cheers Buffy gave me some of that And my 20s and led into the show and casting Alice and had again and it's funny I had one other thing that came up for me from her question was I just watched the Conan O'Brien mark twain prize show on Netflix, which is great Is that where everybody brings their a game and what Ferrell's so funny?
至于情景喜剧嘛，你知道的，让我萌生创作情景喜剧想法的，主要是《欢乐酒店》，《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》也给了我那种感觉，在我二十多岁的时候，一直影响到后来的节目和选角，说来也巧，听了她的问题，我脑海里还浮现出另一件事——我刚在网飞上看了柯南·奥布莱恩获得马克·吐温奖的节目，太棒了，那是大家都拿出看家本领的场合，威尔·法瑞尔怎么能那么搞笑？

01:01:54.119 --> 01:01:58.059
I saw that clip will Ferrell so funny watch it Watch the whole thing.
我看过那个片段，威尔·法瑞尔太搞笑了，快去看，整场节目都看。

01:01:58.059 --> 01:02:00.260
It's all killer, no filler.
全是精华，绝无凑数。

01:02:00.260 --> 01:02:03.980
It's like everybody comes and brings their A-game because they love Conan so much.
就像每个人都倾尽全力，因为他们太爱柯南了。

01:02:03.980 --> 01:02:05.619
And why am I bringing that up?
我为什么提起这个？

01:02:05.619 --> 01:02:14.099
It's because watching that made me realize how much Conan O'Brien has meant to me from 1993 premiering in Dave Letterman's old slot.
因为看了那个节目，我意识到柯南·奥布莱恩对我的意义有多重大，从1993年他在大卫·莱特曼的旧时段开播开始。

01:02:14.099 --> 01:02:15.699
Dave Letterman, a lot to me too.
大卫·莱特曼对我也意义重大。

01:02:15.699 --> 01:02:18.940
I wrote for Dave Letterman a few years after that.
几年后我还为大卫·莱特曼写过剧本。

01:02:18.940 --> 01:02:20.059
I wanted to write for Conan.
我曾想为柯南写剧本。

01:02:20.059 --> 01:02:23.340
Carter and I both really were angling to write for Conan.
卡特和我当时都非常渴望能为柯南写东西。

01:02:23.340 --> 01:02:25.739
And they were not hiring, but Letterman was.
但他们当时不招人，不过莱特曼招。

01:02:25.739 --> 01:02:29.659
incredibly lucky and I love Letterman equally as much in a different way.
我真的极其幸运，而且我对莱特曼的爱同样深厚，只是方式不同。

01:02:29.659 --> 01:02:46.260
But Conan, just watching the Netflix special about his sort of totality of his career, I felt like emotional watching it because I'm like, I just feel like I've lived 30 years with this guy and I got this brief little intersection with him where he came and spoilers, it was a background extra.
但柯南，只是看着网飞关于他整个职业生涯特辑，我看着看着就情绪涌动了，因为我觉得，自己仿佛和这家伙一起度过了30年，我和他有过那么一小段美妙的交集，他曾来客串——剧透一下，他演了一个背景群众演员。

01:02:46.260 --> 01:02:56.139
In an episode of How Much A Mother, we will explain that more when we get to it many years from now and after that, he knew that Carter and I were big fans And he asked us to, we asked him to lunch.
在《老妈老爸浪漫史》的一集里，很多年后的今天，当我们聊到那集时，我们会详细解释，在那之后，他知道卡特和我都是他的超级粉丝，他邀请我们去吃午饭。

01:02:56.139 --> 01:02:59.739
And so a few weeks later, we got to spend a couple hours having lunch with Conan.
于是几周后，我们得以和柯南一起吃午餐，度过了几个小时。

01:02:59.739 --> 01:03:02.619
And it was just, I just had this feeling and watching it.
当时我就有这种感觉，看着那个特辑时也是。

01:03:02.619 --> 01:03:10.099
I think it's what Jessica's talking about, which is you just can't, not many things can give you the feeling that you spent your whole life with somebody.
我想这就是杰西卡所说的，那种你根本无法……没多少东西能给你这种感觉：你仿佛和某个人共度了你的一生。

01:03:10.099 --> 01:03:13.420
You've spent years and years and years in this world or with this energy.
你在这样一个世界里，或者和这样一种能量一起，度过了年复一年又一年。

01:03:13.420 --> 01:03:16.820
And so I'm so happy him, him was that to her.
所以我太高兴了，他对于她来说就是这样的存在。

01:03:16.820 --> 01:03:17.900
What is it for you, Josh?
对你来说呢，乔希？

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:19.300
What comes up to you for you of that?
有什么东西会让你产生这种感觉？

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:24.139
Like, what have I held with me this over cross, across such a long time.
比如，有什么东西是我一直带在身边，历经如此漫长岁月的？

01:03:24.139 --> 01:03:24.980
Yeah.
对。

01:03:24.980 --> 01:03:27.940
Well, we've talked about the movie Sing Street, which is one of my favorite movies.
嗯，我们聊过电影《初恋这首情歌》，那是我最喜欢的电影之一。

01:03:27.940 --> 01:03:28.780
I've just seen some of it.
我刚看了一部分。

01:03:28.780 --> 01:03:29.619
I love that movie.
我太喜欢那部电影了。

01:03:29.619 --> 01:03:31.960
I just re-watched that movie.
我刚重温了那部电影。

01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:33.280
I bet Elliott likes that movie too.
我敢说埃利奥特也会喜欢那部电影。

01:03:33.280 --> 01:03:34.119
Has he seen it?
他看过吗？

01:03:34.119 --> 01:03:34.960
He hasn't seen that.
他没看过。

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:36.280
Yeah, that would be a great one to watch.
对，那会是一部很棒的观赏选择。

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:37.400
What a music movie.
多棒的一部音乐电影啊。

01:03:37.400 --> 01:03:38.840
One of the great music movies.
最棒的音乐电影之一。

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:40.400
And great brother movies.
也是最棒的兄弟情电影之一。

01:03:40.400 --> 01:03:43.159
Yeah, it's just works on all levels for me.
对，它对我和而言在各个层面都很完美。

01:03:43.159 --> 01:03:48.519
I also, you know, I'm a huge fan of the before trilogy, the link letter.
另外，你知道的，我是理查德·林克莱特导演的“爱在”三部曲的超级粉丝。

01:03:48.519 --> 01:03:53.199
See, that's a trilogy, because you have years and years that spans 15 years, those three movies.
你看，那才叫三部曲，因为这三部电影跨越了15年的漫长岁月。

01:03:53.199 --> 01:03:58.559
And you get to watch them age, which is always so moving to watch people get older.
你能看着他们变老，看着人们日渐衰老总是那么触动人心。

01:03:58.559 --> 01:04:02.599
And I just think there's something, he really catches something in those movies.
而且我真的觉得，他在那些电影里捕捉到了一些非常特别的东西。

01:04:02.599 --> 01:04:07.599
The last one's almost like impossible to watch in some ways, because it's so tragic, but also so beautiful.
最后一部在某种程度上几乎让人不忍卒读，因为它太悲伤了，但也美得令人窒息。

01:04:08.519 --> 01:04:10.280
But I love those.
但我爱死它们了。

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:20.852
I'm trying to think, I mean, there's certain authors that I, just I'm always coming back to, I've talked about this before, Nick Cave does this weekly.
我在想，我的意思是，有些作者我总是会一直重读，我之前也说过，尼克·凯夫每周都会写这个。

01:04:20.852 --> 01:04:22.793
It's called the Red Hand Files.
它叫《红手信箱》（The Red Hand Files）。

01:04:22.793 --> 01:04:25.610
And he just answers kind of like reader mail.
他就是回答一些读者的来信。

01:04:25.610 --> 01:04:27.801
And he's the most beautiful writer.
而且他是那种文笔极美的人。

01:04:27.801 --> 01:04:28.928
He's really funny.
他真的很有趣。

01:04:28.928 --> 01:04:32.121
And it's reliably the best thing I read every week.
而且这绝对是我每周读到的最棒的东西。

01:04:32.121 --> 01:04:37.317
And I feel like I get to sit around this campfire with Nick Cave and have him tell.
我觉得自己就像能和尼克·凯夫围坐在篝火旁，听他讲故事。

01:04:37.317 --> 01:04:42.199
He wrote one today that was so funny about him and Nick Cage getting confused.
他今天写了一篇超级好笑的故事，讲的是他和尼克·凯奇名字总被人搞混。

01:04:42.199 --> 01:04:50.199
And he was drinking with this guy whose wife was leaving him and he was like, my wife loves you, man.
当时他在和一个家伙喝酒，那家伙的妻子要跟他离婚，那家伙就说，我老婆超爱你的，老兄。

01:04:50.199 --> 01:04:51.113
I need to tell her.
我得告诉她。

01:04:51.113 --> 01:04:55.199
And he realized he thought he was Nick Cage, the actor, not Nick Cage, the rock star.
然后尼克·凯夫才意识到，对方以为他是演员尼克·凯奇，而不是摇滚明星尼克·凯奇。

01:04:55.199 --> 01:05:08.199
So he ends up like writing an autograph for her and he ends up talking about, you know, like raising Arizona was a tough shoot, but the Coen brothers are so great and he was so drunk, you know, just imitating Nick Cage.
于是他最后只好给她写了张签名，还聊起了什么，你知道的，比如《抚养亚利桑那》（Raising Arizona）拍的时候挺不容易的，但科恩兄弟太棒了，而且他当时喝得大醉，你知道的，就在那儿模仿尼克·凯奇。

01:05:08.199 --> 01:05:50.800
But I love those so much and I think, I don't know, I think the campfires I tend to gravitate around are a little more probably movies or books, but you know, I grew up watching Cheers and Family Ties and, you know, that Thursday lineup, yeah, Nightcord LA Law, I mean, so I feel, I watch so much more TV when I was a kid than I do now, much to my wife, Sugar But, yeah, I mean, I don't think anyone gets in this business that we're in without having things that have really taken up residence in their psyche, you know?
但我太喜欢这些了，我想，我不知道，我觉得我倾向于围坐在一起的篝火，可能更多还是电影或书吧，但你知道的，我从小看《干杯》（Cheers）和《天才家庭》（Family Ties），还有，你知道的，周四晚间的那些黄金档，没错，还有《夜间法庭》（Night Court）、《洛城法律》（LA Law），我是说，所以我小时候看电视的时间可比现在多多了，这让我太太舒格（Sugar）很不——不过，好吧，我的意思是，我觉得没有谁能进入我们这行，却不让某些东西真正在自己的心灵深处占据一席之地的，你知道吗？

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:54.639
And I'm sure we'll end this and I'll think of eight more.
而且我敢肯定，等我们结束录音后，我还能想起另外八个。

01:05:54.639 --> 01:05:57.880
Well, I think you hit the nail on the head by saying the word campfire.
嗯，我觉得你提到“篝火”这个词，真是说到点子上了。

01:05:57.880 --> 01:06:01.639
I think Jessica feels like she was around this campfire with us for all these years.
我觉得杰西卡（Jessica）一定觉得这些年来她就像和我们围坐在同一堆篝火旁一样。

01:06:01.639 --> 01:06:02.639
We're all telling stories.
我们大家都在讲故事。

01:06:02.639 --> 01:06:11.119
And I think it's such a gift to have that campfire kind of burn for as many, for enough years to sort of tell enough stories around.
我觉得能让那堆篝火燃烧这么多年，燃烧足够多的年头，以便能在周围讲上足够多的故事，这真是一份巨大的礼物。

01:06:11.119 --> 01:06:13.260
And I think it's so rare to get to do that.
而且我觉得能做到这一点非常难得。

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:15.880
That's why my brain went to like things I've spent lots of time with.
这就是为什么我的脑海里会浮现出那些我花了很多时间陪伴的事物。

01:06:15.880 --> 01:06:17.559
I think that's what she's talking about.
我想这就是她所说的意思。

01:06:17.559 --> 01:06:19.480
So yeah, thank you, Jessica, for the question.
所以，是的，谢谢杰西卡提出的问题。

01:06:19.480 --> 01:06:23.119
Thank you for spending that much time with us around the campfire.
谢谢你花那么多时间陪我们围坐在篝火旁。

01:06:23.119 --> 01:06:24.800
And this new campfire too.
还有这堆新的篝火。

01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:25.800
So thank you.
所以，谢谢你。

